{"id":"v0-cfo-sceptic-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Errors compound across steps, so an unchecked agent workflow is mostly wrong, and you shouldn't trust a vendor's or the tool's word that a check works. Test candidate checks against your own reference and keep the one whose numbers you can show, and make the control state what it doesn't cover. Nobody told me what any of it costs, though.","claims":["Before trusting any automated check on an agent's multi-step work, run it against a sample where you already know the right answers, pick the check whose scores match them, and write down what that check does not catch. Don't accept a vendor's or tool's claim that a check works.","Before trusting any automated check on an agent's multi-step work, run the candidate checks against a set of cases where you already know the right answer, keep the one that performs best on those cases, and write down what that check does not catch. Don't accept a vendor's or tool's claim that its check works.","Before trusting any automated step in a multi-step agent pipeline, test each candidate check against answers you already know are right, pick the one with measured results you can show, and write down what that check does not catch. Don't accept anyone's claim that a check works, because small errors pile up from step to step.","When an agent runs a multi-step job, each step's mistakes carry into the next, so don't assume the result is right without checking. Don't accept a vendor's or tool's claim that its check works; try several candidate checks against a set of answers you already know are correct, pick the one with measurable results, and write down what that check can't catch."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","none","evaluations","security","security"],"why":["Validating a check against known-answer samples, picking the one whose scores match, and documenting its blind spots is eval/judge calibration (a repeatable, fixed judge); H is about running safety checks, not validating the check itself.","The claim is about validating an automated judge against known answers before relying on it, which is eval practice (a trusted, fixed judge). It is not about safety checks (H) or grounding (C).","Testing candidate checks against known-answer cases and keeping the best is building a fixed, repeatable eval/judge, rather than trusting claims, which is the eval idea.","Testing candidate checks against known-answer cases, keeping the best, and documenting its blind spots describes building a fixed, repeatable eval/judge to measure work, not grounding or safety-checking.","The claim is about validating a check against known-right answers and documenting its blind spots. That is judge calibration, not the eval idea (measure output against fixed criteria to see if a change helped), the run-the-check safety idea, or any of the others.","Testing candidate checks against known-right answers, choosing by measured results, and not trusting claims is the eval idea: a fixed, repeatable judge to tell whether changes improved the work.","The claim's core is that you can't trust a multi-step agent's output without running a check. Choosing among checks and noting their limits is detail around that. It doesn't carry C's idea of a fixed judge measuring whether a change improved the work, and it doesn't carry F's grounding.","The claim is to check the output instead of assuming it is right, and to validate the check itself. It says nothing about grounding, comparing changes with a fixed judge, or the other ideas."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cfo-sceptic-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Unchecked AI output goes wrong and compounds over steps, so you test candidate checks on your own material and keep the one that scores best, and verify the file, not the agent's report. Honestly, it's mostly the compounding arithmetic that stuck; there wasn't one idea I could tie to a cost.","claims":["When an AI does a multi-step job, small mistakes pile up from step to step, so don't trust what the AI says it did. Open the actual output file and check it yourself, and before relying on any checking method, try it on your own real material to see whether it catches problems.","Before trusting any automated check on an AI's work, try several candidate checks against your own real examples, and use whichever one's verdicts best match what you know is right. Then inspect the actual output files yourself, since the AI's own summary of what it did can be wrong.","Before trusting any automated check on your agent's work, try several candidate checks against material you already know the right answer for, and use whichever one catches the most real mistakes. When the agent says a task is done, open the actual output file and inspect it yourself rather than believing the agent's summary.","When an AI does a multi-step job, small errors pile up from step to step, so don't trust what the AI says it did. Open the actual result yourself and check it, and before relying on any check, try a few on your own sample material and use the one that catches the most problems."],"picks":["security","security","output-quality","none","none","security","security","security"],"why":["Don't trust the agent's account of its work; open the output and run the check on your own material — matches 'can't tell safety from output, run the check'.","The claim says not to trust the agent's own account and to check the actual output, and to test any checking method on real material before relying on it. That is A's point: output alone can't tell you the agent is safe, so you run the check.","The claim says to check automated verdicts against your own real examples and the actual output files, not the AI's own summary. That is staying connected to ground truth, which is the grounding idea.","The claim is about choosing a check by testing it against known-right examples, then verifying outputs yourself. That is neither grounding (E), a fixed-judge eval for measuring change (F), nor 'run the check' as a safety practice (A).","The claim is about picking the best checker on known-answer material and opening the output yourself. It never says the agent fills gaps it can't reach, so it isn't D's grounding. It doesn't say to keep a judge fixed to measure change, so it isn't F's eval idea.","The claim is about running and validating a check, and not trusting the agent's own summary of its work. That matches E's 'run the check' idea, not B's idea of staying connected to truth so the gaps stay visible.","The claim says not to trust what the agent reports, to open the result and run a check yourself, and to pick the check that catches the most problems. That is the idea that you can't judge safety from output and have to run the check.","The claim says not to trust the AI's own account of what it did and to open the result and run a check yourself. That is F's point that you can't judge from the output, so you run the check. It never says the agent fills gaps it can't reach, which H requires."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-cfo-sceptic-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"An agent chain gets unreliable fast (ten steps at 85% each is right about 20% of the time), so you need an independent check, not the AI asking itself 'are you sure?'. Pick that check by testing candidates on your own material and reading a scoreboard, then save the winner with its stated limits.","claims":["When you chain many AI steps, errors multiply, so the whole chain is often wrong even if each step is mostly right. Don't rely on the AI re-asking itself whether it's sure; add a separate check that doesn't share its blind spots. Choose that check by trying several options on your own real examples and comparing their scores, then keep the best one and write down where it doesn't work.","When you chain many AI steps, errors multiply: with ten steps that are each about 85% reliable, only about one in five runs comes out right. So don't rely on the AI re-asking itself whether it's sure. Set up a separate checker. To choose it, try several candidate checkers on your own real material, compare how each scores, and keep the best one. Write down what it can and can't catch.","When you chain many AI steps, errors multiply, so don't trust the chain's output on its own, and don't let the same AI grade itself. Set up a separate checker, choose it by trying several candidate checkers on your own real examples and comparing their scores, then keep the best one and write down where it is known to fail.","When you chain many AI steps, errors multiply, so don't trust the chain's output on its own, and don't let the model just re-confirm its own work. Add a separate checker. Choose it by trying several candidate checkers on your own real examples and comparing their scores. Keep the best one and write down where it works and where it doesn't."],"picks":["none","security","security","security","evaluations","evaluations","none","security"],"why":["The claim is about chained errors compounding, adding an independent check, and picking that check empirically. That isn't grounding (B), safety checking (E), or the eval idea (A), which centers on a fixed judge measuring whether a change improved the work.","Chained errors compound, self-checking is unreliable, so add an independent check and run it, which is the 'can't tell safety from output, run the check' idea. The part about picking a check by scoring options on real examples is secondary.","The claim says not to trust the AI's own sense that it's right, and to set up a separate check and run it. That matches 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check.' Picking the best checker is part of building that check, not an eval of whether changes improve the work (G), and nothing in it is about grounding in outside truth (B).","The claim says not to trust the agent's own 'are you sure' and to run a separate checker, which is G's practice of running the check. It doesn't carry A's fixed judge for measuring change, or C's grounding.","The claim is about building a fixed, separate judge, picking it by scoring candidates against your own real examples, and documenting where it fails. That is the eval idea: repeatable measurement with a judge that stays fixed.","Separate fixed checker chosen by comparing candidate judges' scores on your own real examples is eval/judge calibration, not just safety checking (C) or grounding (F).","The claim is about adding an independent checker and picking the best one empirically. That is not grounding (A), safety checks (B), or an eval with a fixed judge that measures whether a change improved the work (G).","The core is that you can't trust the output alone, so you add a separate check and run it. Picking the checker by scoring candidates is a detail, and the claim says nothing about using a fixed judge to measure changes, which is G."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cfo-sceptic-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cfo-sceptic","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't ask the AI whether it's sure; build a reference set with known errors, run several candidate checks against it, and keep the one that scores best on the scoreboard. I still don't know what it costs.","claims":["When you need to trust an AI's output, don't just ask it to confirm it's right. Assemble a set of examples where you already know which answers are wrong, try out several different ways of checking against that set, and use whichever way catches the known errors best.","Don't just ask the AI whether its answer is right. Collect a set of examples where you already know which answers are wrong, try several different ways of checking the AI's work against that set, and adopt the checking method that catches the most known errors.","Before trusting any automated check on your agent's work, build a set of test cases where you already know which outputs are wrong, try several versions of the check on that set, and adopt whichever catches the most known errors. Don't just ask the agent to confirm its own work.","Don't just ask the AI to confirm its own work. Assemble a set of examples where you already know which answers are wrong, try several different ways of checking against that set, and use whichever one catches the most known errors."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations"],"why":["Build a labeled set of known errors, compare checking methods against it, keep the best one: that is a fixed, repeatable measurement of whether the judge works, which is the eval idea, not just 'run a check' (C).","A labeled set of known-wrong answers, comparing several checking methods against it, and adopting the best is the eval idea: measure output against criteria repeatably to tell what works.","Building a known-error test set, comparing judge versions on it, and adopting the best one is the eval idea: a repeatable, fixed judge measured against criteria.","Building a known-error test set, comparing check versions on it, and adopting the best one is eval practice: measuring output against defined criteria with a judge you validate and then keep fixed.","Building a known-error set, comparing several checking methods and keeping the one that catches the known errors best is the eval practice: a fixed, repeatable measure of the output against criteria you define.","The claim is about building a labeled set of known errors and testing which checker catches them best. That means measuring output against defined criteria with a validated judge, which is the eval idea. It carries nothing about grounding in truth (G) or about safety (H).","Known-wrong example set, compare several checking methods, keep the one catching most errors = a repeatable eval with a fixed judge to measure whether changes improve work.","A known-error test set, several candidate checks tried, and keeping the one that catches the most is a repeatable, fixed-judge eval that tells you whether the work got better."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cto-builder-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"no single check catches everything, so run a few different ones against your own output, score them on real data, and keep the winner as a reusable judge file that says what it misses. the winner wasn't the one i'd have guessed, so don't just trust your gut that the output is fine.","claims":["Don't rely on a single way of checking your AI's work. Build several different checks, test each one on real examples where you already know what good looks like, and pick whichever performs best, which may surprise you. Save that check as a file you can rerun, with a note on what kinds of problems it fails to catch. Don't assume the output is fine just because it looks fine to you.","When you need to know whether your output is good, don't rely on one test or on your own impression. Build several different tests, run each on real examples, and compare how well they perform. Keep the best one and save it as a reusable reviewer document that also lists the kinds of problems it fails to catch. Expect the best test to be a surprise.","When you need to know whether your agent's output is good, don't rely on one test. Write several different tests, run each against real examples of the output, and see which one best separates good from bad. Save the best test as a reusable file that also states what it fails to detect. Expect the best test not to be the one you would have picked, so check with data instead of assuming the output is fine.","When you need to know whether your agent's output is good, don't rely on one test. Build several different tests, run each on real examples, and compare how well each one matches reality. Keep the best performer as a saved, reusable reviewer file that also lists what it fails to catch. Expect the best test to surprise you, so measure instead of trusting your own impression."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","security","evaluations"],"why":["Build multiple candidate tests, run them on real examples, measure which matches reality, keep the best as a fixed reusable judge: that is the eval idea of repeatable measurement against a fixed judge.","Building multiple tests, running them on real examples, comparing them to reality, and keeping the best as a fixed reusable judge is about measuring output quality repeatably, which is the eval idea.","Building candidate tests, validating them on real outputs, and saving the best as a reusable fixed judge file is the eval idea (measure output against criteria repeatably).","Writing multiple candidate tests, running them on real examples, picking the best by data, and saving it as a reusable file is the eval idea: a repeatable, fixed judge that measures output quality.","Build several judges, test them on real examples, keep the best as a reusable reviewer with known blind spots: this is eval measurement against criteria with a fixed judge, which is G. The checks in E are about safety, not output quality.","Building several tests, running them on real examples, comparing performance, and keeping the best as a reusable reviewer (judge) is the eval idea: measuring output repeatably with a fixed judge to know whether work got better.","The core is that output looking fine proves nothing, so you build and run a validated check. The claim has no eval framing of a fixed judge used to compare changes (C), and no grounding or truth-source framing (D).","Build several checks, test them against known-good examples, keep the best as a rerunnable file with documented blind spots. That is building a repeatable, fixed judge so you can measure output quality, which is the eval idea. H is similar, but the claim's substance is calibrating and fixing a judge, not just running a safety check."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cto-builder-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"One check isn't enough: race several candidate checks on your own data, score each one, and save the winner as a judge file that states what it misses, because each check misses different failures and even a well-equipped agent still invents things.","claims":["When you need to verify an AI agent's work, don't trust one test. Build several different tests, run them all on your own real examples, and score how well each catches the mistakes. Keep the best-scoring test and write it into a reusable file that also lists the kinds of mistakes it can't catch, because every test has blind spots and the agent can still make things up even when it has good tools.","When one automated check of the agent's work isn't enough, build several different checks, run them all on a sample of your own real material, and score how well each catches problems. Keep the best one in a file that also lists the kinds of failures it tends to miss. Don't assume the agent is reliable just because it has good tools and context; it can still make things up, so verify its output.","When a single automated check can't be trusted to catch everything, run several different checks against your own real examples, grade how well each one does, and keep the best performer in a saved file that also lists the kinds of mistakes it fails to catch. Assume the AI will still make things up even when it has good tools, so never rely on one check alone.","When you need to verify an agent's work, don't rely on a single test. Build several different tests, run them all against your own real examples, and score how well each one catches errors. Keep the best-scoring one as a standing reviewer document that also lists the kinds of mistakes it fails to catch. Assume the agent will still make things up even when it has good tools and context."],"picks":["security","security","evaluations","security","evaluations","none","evaluations","security"],"why":["The claim is about how to verify an agent's work: build checks, score how well each catches mistakes, keep the best, and record its blind spots. That matches 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check, not waiting for certainty'. It isn't H, because it's about choosing a trustworthy check, not comparing versions with a fixed judge.","The claim's action is to build and run checks on the agent's work instead of trusting it. Its choosing of the best test and its noting that the agent can still make things up don't match F's fixed judge or G's grounding idea.","Building several checks, scoring them on your own real material, and keeping the best one with its known blind spots is the eval idea: repeatable measurement against a fixed judge.","The actionable core is to build and run checks on the agent's output instead of trusting it. The claim never states grounding as staying connected to truth, and it doesn't describe a fixed judge for measuring change.","The colleague would build several checks, grade each against real examples, and keep the best one as a fixed, repeatable judge. That is eval practice. The claim says to assume the AI will make things up, but the action it prescribes is judge selection, not staying connected to ground truth (D).","The claim is about running several checks, grading them and keeping the best one. It never says to stay connected to ground truth, so it doesn't carry C's grounding idea. It also doesn't carry A's fixed judge for measuring whether a change improved the work, or D's idea of running a check instead of waiting for certainty.","Build several tests, score each on real examples, keep the best as a fixed judge with known blind spots — that is the eval idea (repeatable measurement with a fixed judge); the closing 'makes things up' line is a side note.","The claim is about checking an agent's work with tests you build and run, not by trusting its output. It says nothing about staying connected to truth (D) or about measuring a change with a fixed judge (E)."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cto-builder-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"dont guess which check catches your agent's mistakes, race a few on your own output with planted fake claims and keep the winner. my gut pick missed stuff another one caught, so 'the output looks fine' doesnt hold up.","claims":["When you need to pick a check for catching your agent's errors, don't choose one by instinct. Test several candidate checks against the agent's real output after deliberately inserting made-up false statements, see which one catches them, and use that one. Don't trust the output just because it reads as fine.","When you need to pick a way of verifying your AI agent's work, don't choose by instinct. Take a few candidate verification methods, run them all on the same agent output after you've deliberately inserted known false statements, see which ones catch the inserted errors, and use the best catcher. Don't accept output just because it looks fine.","When you need to pick a way to verify your AI agent's work, don't choose by instinct. Take a few candidate checks, run each on the same agent output after deliberately inserting made-up false statements, see which ones catch them, and use the best performer. Don't accept 'it looks fine' as proof the output is correct.","When choosing a way to check your agent's work, don't pick one by instinct. Take a handful of candidate checks, run each on the same real output where you've deliberately inserted a few false statements, see which ones catch them, and use the best performer. Don't accept 'it looks fine' as proof, because a check you trusted can miss errors that another one finds."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","evaluations"],"why":["The claim says not to trust output because it reads fine and to run a check that catches planted errors. That matches H's point that you can't judge safety from output and must run the check. It never mentions grounding or the agent filling gaps with confident falsehoods (G).","The claim says not to trust output because it reads fine and to run a check that is proven to catch errors. That matches 'you can't tell if it's safe by looking; run the check'. It doesn't mention grounding or a fixed judge.","The claim says not to accept 'it looks fine' and to run checks and see what they catch. That is G's idea that you can't judge output by looking and have to run a check. It says nothing about staying connected to truth (D) or about a fixed judge measuring change (E).","Testing candidate checks by planting false statements and not trusting 'looks fine' is about running a check to verify output safety/correctness rather than judging by looking at it.","The claim says not to trust output that looks fine and to run actual checks, which is F's core. Choosing the checker by planting known errors is a method detail on top of that. It doesn't convey D's grounding idea or B's fixed-judge eval.","The claim rests on not trusting output that looks fine and running a check against planted errors, which is D. Choosing the best catcher is an extra step beyond D, but no other idea fits better.","The claim says 'it looks fine' isn't proof and the answer is to run checks and see which ones catch planted errors. That is E's 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'. It doesn't cover F's fixed judge or measuring a change, and it says nothing about B's grounding.","The claim is about testing candidate checks against a known set of planted errors to pick the best one and measure repeatably. That is the eval idea (judge criteria, comparing changes), not B's 'run a check, don't wait for certainty' or H's grounding."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-cto-builder-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"cto-builder","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust one check: run several detectors on your own output and benchmark which catches what, because they miss different things and 'looked fine' isn't a check. Also, a judge file that states its own limits is something you can hand to other teams.","claims":["When you check your agent's output, don't rely on a single check. Run several different checks on the same output, test which problems each one finds and which it misses, and combine them. Also write down what your checker can and cannot catch, so other teams can safely reuse it.","Don't rely on a single way of checking your agent's work. Run several different checks on the same output, compare what each one catches and misses, and use the combination. Write down the limits of whichever check you use, so other teams can reuse it knowing what it can't do.","When you check your agent's output, don't rely on a single check. Run several different checks on the same output, test which kinds of errors each one catches, and combine them. Write down in the checker's file what it can and can't catch, so other teams can safely reuse it.","When you want to know whether your agent's work is good, don't rely on a single automated checker or on 'it seemed fine'. Run several different checkers on the same output, compare which problems each one finds and which it misses, and keep the mix. Also write down, in the evaluation file itself, what it can and can't judge, so other teams can safely reuse it."],"picks":["none","none","security","none","security","none","none","none"],"why":["It's about using several different checks and documenting each check's limits. E is about needing to run a check at all, and D is about a fixed judge measuring change, so neither matches.","The claim is about layering several different checks and documenting each one's limits. G is about one fixed-judge eval for measuring change, and H is about running a check instead of trusting output. Neither covers combining checks or recording their blind spots.","The claim is about checking agent output with several checks and knowing what each catches and misses. That is the 'you need a way to check' idea. It does not carry the fixed judge or the change-over-time measurement of G.","The claim is about layering several checkers and documenting each one's blind spots. C is about running a check at all, and D is about one fixed judge measuring change, so neither carries that.","The claim is about running several checks on agent output and documenting what each catches, which fits 'you need a check, and the practice is running it'. It says nothing about grounding in truth (E) or a fixed judge measuring change (H).","The claim is about combining several checks and documenting each checker's coverage. That is not grounding (E), and it is not C's point that you must run a check instead of judging safety from the output. It is also not H's fixed judge.","The claim is about using a mix of several checkers to judge quality and documenting their limits. That isn't H's fixed judge measuring change over time, C's safety checks, or grounding in G.","The claim is about using several different checkers, comparing what each misses, and documenting an eval's scope for reuse. B is about one fixed judge measuring change over time, and A is about checking safety, so neither carries that idea."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-hr-svp-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust an AI's output just because it sounds sure; it sounds equally confident whether or not it has a source, so you need a separate check, and you pick which check by testing a few on your own material. A person should still review before the agent acts, and I'd take that review role to leadership.","claims":["An AI's confident tone tells you nothing about whether it is right, since it sounds just as sure with or without a source behind it. So before relying on its output, run an independent check on it. Choose which check to use by trying several on your own real material and keeping the one that works best. Also have a person look it over before the agent takes any action.","An AI's confident tone tells you nothing about whether its output is true, so before relying on its work, run a separate verification step. Choose which verification to use by trying a few on your own data and keeping the one that catches the most problems. Also keep a person reviewing before the AI takes any action, and assign that review job to senior management.","Before an AI agent's output is used or acted on, run an independent check on it; its confident tone tells you nothing about whether it has a source. Try several candidate checks on your own material to see which works best, and have someone with authority review the result before the agent acts.","Before you let an AI agent act on something, add a separate verification step, since its tone sounds just as sure whether or not it is grounded in a real source. Pick that step by trying several candidate checks on your own data and keeping the one that works best. Also have a human review the output before the agent acts, and put that review with senior management."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["Confident tone doesn't show whether the output is right, so you run an independent check on it. That is D's 'can't tell from output, run the check', not H's grounding in a source.","The claim says not to trust an output because it sounds confident, and to run an independent check before relying on it. That is G's idea of running the check. D's idea is grounding the agent in sources.","The claim says output and tone can't show whether the work is sound, so you should run a separate check. That is G's idea (run the check, don't trust the output). It does not say the agent should stay connected to ground truth, which is D.","The claim says you can't judge from the output, even a confident one, so run a separate check. That is the core of C. It doesn't say to stay connected to ground truth, which is what D teaches.","The actionable core is to run an independent check and have someone with authority review it before the agent acts, instead of trusting how the output looks. That matches G's idea of running the check. It doesn't say to connect the agent to ground truth, which is D's point.","The claim says to run an independent check because the agent's confident output can't show it is safe or sourced. That matches B. It isn't F, because it describes no fixed judge or repeatable criteria. It isn't H, because it describes no connection to ground truth.","The claim says you can't judge from the output's tone, so you need a separate check. That is D's 'run the check' idea. It never says to connect the agent to a real source or make the gap visible, which is what G teaches.","The claim's action is to add a separate check because the output's tone can't tell you whether it's safe to act on, which is the run-the-check idea. It never says to connect the agent to real sources or make the gap visible, which is what G would require."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-hr-svp-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"An AI sounds just as sure when it's making things up as when it has a source, so \"are you sure?\" proves nothing. You have to test checks on your own material and keep the one that works, and where an action touches people, a person checks the evidence before the agent acts.","claims":["Don't trust an AI's confidence, since it sounds equally certain whether it's inventing or sourcing, so asking it to confirm proves nothing. Try several different verification methods on your own data and keep whichever one actually catches errors. For any action that affects people, have a human review the evidence before the agent proceeds.","A confident reply from an AI tells you nothing about whether it's right, so don't ask it to confirm itself. Try several different ways of checking its work against your own real data, adopt whichever one actually catches errors, and whenever the agent's action will affect people, have a human review the evidence before the agent proceeds.","Don't trust an AI's confidence, because it sounds equally certain whether it's inventing something or working from a real source, so asking it to confirm itself tells you nothing. Try several different ways of checking its output on your own data and keep whichever one actually catches errors. When its action affects people, have a human look at the evidence before it goes ahead.","Don't trust an AI's own confidence or a 'are you sure?' follow-up, because it sounds equally certain whether it's inventing or sourcing. Try several verification methods on your own data, keep whichever one actually catches errors, and when the agent's action affects people, have a human review the evidence before the agent proceeds."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["The claim says you can't judge reliability from the agent's confident output or its own confirmation, so you have to run an independent check. A would instead tell the colleague to keep the agent connected to ground truth.","The claim says output and confidence can't show whether the agent is right, so you have to run a check (several methods, keep what catches errors, human review for actions that affect people). It never says to stay connected to the truth, which is D's point.","Confident output can't show safety/correctness, so run independent checks against real data and have a human review evidence before agent actions that affect people — that is 'you need a way to check, run the check'.","The claim says a confident reply is no evidence of being right, so you run an independent check (and add human review when people are affected). That is F's idea of running the check. It never says the agent fills gaps when it can't reach the truth, so it isn't H.","The colleague would act by running checks on real data and putting a human review in front of consequential actions, because the output and the agent's confidence can't show whether it is safe. That is the run-the-check idea in G. H is about staying connected to ground truth, and the claim doesn't say that.","The claim says output and confidence can't tell you whether the agent is right, so you run checks and have a human review the evidence. That is D. It never says to connect the agent to a real source, which is what E would add.","The claim says output and self-reported confidence can't show whether the agent is right. Its practice is to run checks, keep the one that catches errors, and add human review of the evidence for high-stakes actions.","Can't judge safety/correctness from the agent's output or its self-confidence; you need to run an actual check (and human review for consequential actions) rather than rely on certainty."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-hr-svp-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust AI output, and don't trust a quality check either, until you've tested it on your own material and scored it against a reference. The AI sounds just as sure when it's wrong, and asking 'are you sure?' proves nothing. Honestly, it was mostly an engineer's afternoon, and I'm not sure what it means for my people.","claims":["Before relying on an AI's answer or on an automated check of its answers, run it on a sample of your own real work where you already know the right result, and compare. Don't treat the AI's confidence, or its reply to 'are you sure?', as evidence it's right.","Before relying on an AI's answer or on an automated check of its answers, run it on a sample of your own real work and compare its results with answers you already know are right. Its confidence tells you nothing, and asking it to confirm itself doesn't count as a test.","Before relying on an AI's answer or on an automated check of its answers, run it on a sample of your own real work and compare the results to answers you already know are right. Don't accept the AI's confidence, or its reply when you ask if it's sure, as evidence that it's correct.","Before you rely on an AI's answer or on an automated check of it, run it on a sample of your own real work and compare the results to answers you already know are correct. Don't take the AI's confidence, or its reply when you ask whether it's sure, as evidence that it's right."],"picks":["security","security","security","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","output-quality","security"],"why":["Confidence and 'are you sure?' replies can't show the AI is right, so you run a check against cases where you know the answer. That is G's 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'.","Run the check on known-answer real work rather than trusting output or confidence; the practice is running the check, not waiting for certainty.","The claim is about running a check on real work against known-right answers instead of trusting output or self-confirmation, which matches 'you need a way to check; the practice is running the check.'","Test the AI/judge on your own real work against known-right answers, not its confidence or self-confirmation: a repeatable eval with fixed criteria and a validated judge.","Testing an AI or automated check against your own real work and known-correct answers is the eval idea: fixed criteria and a fixed judge, repeatable measurement. B is close (don't trust output, run a check), but the claim's substance is calibrating against known-correct answers.","Testing the AI's output on your own real work against known-correct answers, repeatably, is the eval idea. A is about safety checks specifically, and F is about grounding in external truth.","The claim says to check the AI against known-correct answers and not to trust its confidence. That is grounding in truth, since a model that can't reach the ground sounds confident and is wrong.","The claim says you can't judge an AI's answer by its confidence or by asking if it's sure. You have to run a check against known-right answers, which is C's 'run the check' idea."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-hr-svp-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"hr-svp","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"The agent sounds just as sure whether it is right or wrong, so asking it 'are you sure?' proves nothing. The check has to be a separate, written-down thing with known limits, and for anything that matters a person verifies it against the source before the agent acts. The middle hour was mostly tool mechanics for me, but that idea I'd take to my leadership team.","claims":["Don't trust the agent's own confidence or ask it to re-confirm its answer, because it sounds equally certain when wrong. Set up the check as a separate, written procedure with known limitations, and for anything important have a person compare the result to the original source before the agent is allowed to act on it.","Don't treat the agent's own confirmation as a check, because it sounds equally confident when right and when wrong. Build the check as a separate, documented step with stated limits, and for important work have a person compare the result to the original source before the agent is allowed to act.","Don't trust an AI agent's own confidence or its answer to 'are you sure?', because it sounds equally certain when it's wrong. Set up a separate, written-down check whose limits you know, and for anything important have a person compare the result against the original source before the agent is allowed to act.","When the agent produces something important, don't trust it just because it sounds confident or because it says it's sure when you ask. Set up a separate, written check with stated limits, and have a person compare the output against the original source before the agent is allowed to act on it."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["Separate written check run by a person against the source, instead of trusting agent confidence, matches 'can't tell safety from output; run the check'.","Output can't prove safety (confidence is equally certain when wrong), so run a separate, written check with human verification against source — the 'run the check' idea, not grounding (C).","The claim says the agent's self-confirmation isn't a check and calls for running a separate, documented, human-verified check; this matches 'you can't tell safety from output, run the check' rather than grounding's 'stay connected to truth'.","Agent's self-confirmation isn't a check; build a separate, documented check run before acting — 'can't tell safety from output, run the check'.","Can't judge safety/correctness from the agent's output or its self-confidence; the practice is running a separate, known-limits check rather than trusting the output.","Output (including the agent's confidence) can't show safety; run a separate, known-limits check and verify before acting.","The claim is about not trusting the agent's own output or its stated confidence, and acting on a separate written check that is actually run. That is H's 'can't tell by looking at output, run the check'. E is about keeping the agent grounded in truth, not about verifying its output.","Claim says confidence and self-reported certainty aren't evidence of safety; run a separate written check instead. That is B's 'can't tell from output, run the check'."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-ingrid-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust one check or the agent's own summary: run several checkers against planted errors on your own material, score them, and keep a judge file that says what it catches and what it misses. 'Grounded' only means traceable to a source, not correct.","claims":["When you need to know whether an AI's work is right, don't accept one reviewer or the AI's own report. Plant known mistakes in your own real material, have several separate reviewers look for them, score how many each finds, and keep a written note of what each reviewer reliably catches and misses. Also remember that 'it points back to a source' does not mean 'it is true'; check the claim itself.","When you need to trust an agent's work, don't accept its own report or a single review. Plant known mistakes in your own material, have several separate reviewers look for them, and score how many each one finds. Keep a written note for each reviewer listing what it reliably finds and what it misses. Also remember that 'it points to a source' only shows where a claim came from, not that the claim is right.","When you rely on an AI agent to review work, don't accept a single review or the agent's own report of how it went. Plant known mistakes in your own real material, run several different reviewers on it, and score how many planted mistakes each one finds. Keep a written note for each reviewer listing what kinds of errors it reliably catches and what it misses. Also, don't treat 'every statement points back to a source' as proof the statement is right; a claim can cite a real source and still be wrong.","When an AI agent tells you its work is good, don't accept it. Have several separate reviewers examine it, after you've hidden known mistakes in your own real material. Score how many of those mistakes each reviewer finds, and keep a written note for each reviewer listing what it reliably finds and what it misses. Also, 'it points back to a source' only shows where a claim came from, not that it is right, so check the claim itself."],"picks":["security","none","security","security","security","evaluations","security","security"],"why":["Planting known mistakes, scoring several reviewers, and not trusting self-report or 'points to a source' is about running a check because output alone can't show safety or correctness.","The claim is about verifying agent work with planted errors, multiple reviewers and per-reviewer notes. That is closest to G, but it's a verification method rather than G's point that you can't judge safety from output. F's fixed judge is a different idea, and the final 'points to a source' line is a B-like point about grounding.","The claim is about not trusting the output or the AI's own report and instead running a check (planted flaws, several separate reviewers, scored catches). That is 'you can't tell by looking at output, run the check'. The source-versus-truth line is a side note.","The claim says not to trust the AI's own report and to run a check (planted mistakes, several reviewers, scored catch rates) instead of waiting for certainty. That matches 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'. It is not about comparing versions with a fixed judge (E), and the source-pointing remark is only a side note.","The core action is not trusting the agent's own report or a single review, and instead running a check (planted known errors, scored per reviewer). That is 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'.","Planting known mistakes, scoring several reviewers, and keeping notes on each reviewer's catch profile is evaluating output against fixed criteria repeatably; the closing citation point is a side note, not the main idea.","The claim says not to trust an agent's own 'it's good' and to run a check instead: seed known mistakes and score how many each reviewer catches. That matches 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'. It is not E, because the claim is about testing the reviewers, not measuring whether a change improved the work.","The claim says not to trust the agent's own report that its work is good and to run a check: separate reviewers tested against mistakes you planted. That is 'you can't tell from the output, run the check', not an eval of whether a change improved the work (F) or grounding (E)."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-ingrid-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust an AI briefing or a multi-step workflow without a check, and don't take advice on which check to use: build a small test from your own sources with planted errors and see which check actually catches them. You end up with a reusable judge file that says what it catches and where it stops.","claims":["Before you rely on an AI-written summary or a chained set of AI steps, make a small practice set from your own documents with mistakes you inserted on purpose. Try several ways of checking and see which one actually finds those mistakes, rather than taking someone's recommendation. Save the winning check as a reusable file that states which kinds of errors it finds and which it misses.","Before relying on any AI-written summary or chained multi-step process, make a small test set from your own documents with deliberately inserted mistakes, run each candidate checking method on it, and pick the one that actually finds the mistakes. Don't go by what anyone recommends. Save the result as a reusable file that records which mistakes that check finds and which it misses.","Before relying on an AI-written summary or a chain of automated steps, make a small practice set from your own documents with mistakes you deliberately inserted, then run each candidate check against it and keep the one that actually finds the inserted mistakes. Don't pick a check because someone recommends it. Save the winning check as a reusable file that notes what kinds of mistakes it finds and which ones it misses.","Before relying on any AI-written summary or chain of automated steps, make a small practice set from your own documents with mistakes you inserted on purpose. Run each candidate way of checking it against that set and keep the one that actually finds the mistakes, rather than the one someone recommends. Save the result as a reusable checker file that states which kinds of errors it finds and which it misses."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","security","evaluations","security","evaluations"],"why":["Build a test set with known inserted errors, run candidate checks, pick by measured results, and save a reusable record. That is a repeatable eval with a fixed judge, not just 'run the check' (F).","The claim is about building a fixed test set with known mistakes, scoring each checking method against it, saving it for reuse, and choosing by measured results. That is an eval, not a statement that safety can't be judged from output.","Building a seeded-error practice set, comparing checks against it, and saving the winning judge as a reusable file is the eval idea: criteria you define, repeatable, fixed judge.","Building a seeded-error test set, comparing checks against it, and saving the winning check as a reusable fixed judge is the eval idea: repeatable measurement against criteria you define.","Seeding known mistakes and running candidate checks to see which actually catches them is about running a check rather than trusting output; the claim is about verifying (the check), not about measuring change quality against a fixed judge (C) or grounding.","Building a seeded-mistake test set, running candidate checks against it, keeping the winner, and saving it as a reusable file is the eval idea (repeatable measurement with a fixed judge), not safety-checking (A) or grounding (F).","The claim is about not trusting AI output on faith: you build a check, test it on planted errors, and keep the one that catches them as a reusable checker. That is 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'. It doesn't describe measuring whether a change improved the work.","The claim is about building a test set with known errors, testing candidate checks on it, and saving a fixed, reusable checker file. That matches an eval: criteria you define, a judge that stays fixed, and repeatable measurement."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-ingrid-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't ask the model if it's sure. Run several different checks on your own output and score them against known errors, because each check misses different things. Then save the best one as a judge file that says what it catches and misses, so a teammate can rerun it.","claims":["Don't rely on asking the AI whether it's confident in its work. Instead, build several different tests of your own, measure each against mistakes you already know about, and keep the strongest one as a saved reusable file that records which mistakes it finds and which it overlooks, so a coworker can run it again.","Don't settle for asking the AI whether its work is right. Build several different tests of your own, measure each against mistakes you already know about, since each test catches different problems. Keep the best test as a saved file that states what it does and doesn't catch, so a teammate can run it again.","When you want to know if an AI's work is correct, don't just ask it to confirm. Build several separate tests of its output, try them on cases where you already know the mistakes, and see which test catches the most. Keep the best test as a saved file that notes what it finds and what it misses, so a coworker can rerun it later.","Don't trust a model's own 'yes, I'm sure'. Build a handful of different tests for its work, try them on cases where you already know the mistakes, and see which tests catch which ones. Then keep the best test as a saved file that states what it finds and what it overlooks, so a teammate can run it again."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","security","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","none"],"why":["Building multiple tests, measuring each against known mistakes, keeping the best as a fixed, reusable judge file is the eval idea: repeatable criteria and a fixed judge to tell whether changes improve the work.","Building several tests, measuring each against known mistakes, and saving the best as a reusable judge file is the eval idea: repeatable measurement with a fixed judge.","The claim says not to trust the AI's own confidence and to build and validate checks against known mistakes. That is F's 'you need a way to check'. It has nothing about comparing changes with a fixed judge (H) or about grounding (D).","Building your own tests, measuring each against known mistakes, and keeping the strongest as a reusable, shareable file is the eval idea: criteria you define, repeatable, with a fixed judge, and not self-reported confidence.","Building several tests, trying them on known mistakes, keeping the best as a reusable saved judge is the eval idea: repeatable measurement against fixed criteria, not just asking the agent to confirm.","Build several tests, try them on known mistakes, keep the best as a fixed, rerunnable file: this is an eval with a fixed judge, repeatable and checked against known cases.","Building several tests, trying them on known mistakes, and keeping the best as a reusable saved judge with documented scope is the eval idea: criteria defined, repeatable, fixed judge. A is about running a check rather than trusting output, but the claim goes further by comparing tests and saving a reusable one.","The claim is about picking and saving a reusable check. It never says to stay connected to ground truth (A). It doesn't say to measure whether a change improved the work (G), and it isn't about telling safety from output (H)."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-ingrid-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ingrid","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't pick a checking method on someone's say-so: run several on your own output against a known answer, and let a scorer show which catches what. Fluent isn't the same as sourced, so you can't judge by tone.","claims":["Before trusting any way of verifying an AI's work, test several candidate methods on your own output where you already know the right answer, and score them to see which method finds which kinds of errors. Don't judge by how confident or polished the text sounds, because well-written text can still lack backing from real sources.","Don't trust any single way of checking AI output just because someone recommends it. Take a case where you already know the right answer, run several different checks on the AI's output, and use a scorer to see which check catches which errors. Also, text that sounds smooth and confident is not necessarily backed by a real source, so never judge reliability by how convincing it sounds.","When you need to verify AI output, don't trust anyone's recommendation for which verification technique to use. Try several techniques on your own AI's output where you already know the right answer, and see which techniques actually catch the errors. Also, text that sounds smooth and confident may not be backed by a real source, so never judge accuracy by how convincing it sounds.","Don't trust anyone's claim about which way of checking AI output works best. Take a case where you already know the right answer, run each candidate check on the AI's output, and use a separate scoring step to see what each one catches and misses. Also, don't treat smooth, confident wording as proof that a statement is backed by a real source."],"picks":["evaluations","none","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","evaluations","evaluations"],"why":["The action is to score candidate checking methods against known-answer output, which is building and validating a repeatable eval. The polish and grounding remarks are only a caution.","The action is to benchmark several verification methods on known-answer output and keep the best. That is the old 'run several checks, keep the winner' idea. It isn't grounding (staying connected to real sources), eval-as-change-measurement, or run-the-check safety. The closing line about polished text lacking sources only touches grounding.","The claim is about accuracy of AI output and warns that smooth, confident text may have no real source behind it. That matches grounding: the agent fills gaps confidently, plausibly, wrong. The 'try several techniques, keep what works' part matches the old M5 page wording.","The claim's closing point is that smooth, confident text may have no real source behind it, which is the grounding idea of an agent filling the gap 'confidently, plausibly, wrong'. The advice to test verification techniques on known answers is a method for checking against ground truth, not an eval with a fixed judge (A) or the 'run the check' safety idea (E).","The claim ends on the grounding idea: fluent, confident text isn't backed by a real source, so don't judge reliability by how convincing it sounds. The several-checks part is a method for verifying, not the core idea.","The claim ends on fluent, confident text not being backed by a real source, which is the grounding idea of an agent filling gaps confidently and wrongly. The 'run several checks and see which catches errors' part is how the module teaches it, not a separate idea.","The main action is to run each candidate check against a known-answer case and compare them with a separate fixed scoring step. That is eval practice. The confident-wording line is only a side note, and it doesn't carry the grounding idea of staying connected to the truth.","The main action is to test candidate checks on a known-answer case and score them with a separate fixed judge, which is the eval idea. The line about confident wording is only a side note and doesn't carry grounding as the idea (B)."],"score":0.5,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-ops-director-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Try several checks on the same AI output and pick the best one by the score table, not by opinion. AI sounds just as sure when it is wrong, so you cannot trust it by asking 'are you sure'.","claims":["When an AI's work could be wrong, don't ask it to confirm itself. Run multiple separate verification passes on the same output, score each one in a written comparison table, and choose the result with the best score rather than going on gut feeling, because the AI sounds equally confident whether it is right or wrong.","When you want an AI's work checked, run several different checks on the same output, score each one in a table, and choose the winner from those scores rather than from your own gut feeling. Don't ask the AI whether it is certain, because it sounds equally confident when it is wrong.","When you have an AI output you doubt, run multiple separate checks on it and compare their results in a scored table, then choose the output that scores highest. Don't ask the AI to confirm itself, because it sounds equally confident whether it's right or wrong.","When you have several ways to check an AI's work, run them all on the same output, compare their scores in a table, and choose the one with the best score instead of going by your own impression. Don't treat the AI saying it is confident, or being asked to confirm itself, as evidence that it is right."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","output-quality","security","evaluations","security"],"why":["Don't trust the AI's self-confirmation (confident whether right or wrong); run separate checks and score them. This is the 'run the check' safety idea, not grounding or fixed-judge evals.","Claim says don't trust AI self-confirmation; run repeated checks and score them rather than relying on confidence. That is idea F's 'can't tell by looking at output, need a way to check, run the check'.","The claim says to run checks instead of trusting the output or the AI's own confidence. That matches 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'. It says nothing about staying connected to ground truth (E) or about a fixed judge for measuring change (D).","The claim says the AI's confidence tells you nothing about whether it is right, so you run checks instead of asking if it's certain. That is G. It doesn't mention a fixed judge or tracking whether changes improve the work, so it isn't C, and it has no grounding or ground-truth idea, so it isn't B.","The claim is about distrusting self-reported confidence because the AI sounds equally confident when wrong, and about checking against independent sources, which matches grounding. B is the near miss: it is about running a check on safety, whereas this claim's core is confident-but-wrong output.","The claim says confident output can't be trusted and tells you to run independent checks instead of asking the AI to confirm itself. That matches 'you can't tell from the output, run the check'. It says nothing about grounding in truth (H) or a fixed judge that measures changes (A).","Running several checks on the same output, scoring them in a table and picking by score rather than impression describes a repeatable, fixed-criteria eval; the claim doesn't address grounding or safety.","The claim says to run the checks instead of going by impression or the AI's own confidence. That matches 'you can't tell by looking at the output, run the check'. It doesn't mention a fixed judge or repeatable measurement (A), or staying connected to ground truth (G)."],"score":0.125,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-ops-director-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"The AI makes up things and sounds just as sure as when it is right, so asking 'are you sure' does not help. You need to check against a source file or test your checks and keep the best one.","claims":["When the AI gives an answer, don't ask it whether it's sure, because it sounds equally confident when wrong. Instead check its output against a real source file, or run a test that can fail, and use that result to decide what to trust.","When the AI gives you facts, don't ask it whether it's certain, because it will sound confident whether or not it's right. Compare its output against a real source such as a file or a test result. Build several such checks, run them, and keep whichever one catches the most errors.","When the AI gives you an answer, don't ask it to confirm itself, because it will sound equally certain whether it's right or wrong. Instead, compare its output against a real source such as a reference file, or run a test that can fail, and keep whichever version passes.","When the AI tells you something, don't ask it to confirm itself; it will sound equally certain whether it's right or wrong. Instead, compare its output against a real source such as a reference file, or run a test. If you build several checks, test them against known cases and keep only the one that catches the errors."],"picks":["output-quality","security","security","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The claim says the AI sounds equally certain right or wrong, so check it against a real source (the ground truth) instead of asking it to confirm itself. That is the grounding idea.","The claim says not to trust the output or the AI's self-confirmation, and to run an external check instead (test it on known cases). That matches D's 'run the check' practice. It doesn't say to connect the agent to ground truth or make the gap visible, which is B.","The claim says you can't judge the output by how confident it sounds, so you run a check that can fail and trust the result. That is idea A. It doesn't say the agent fills gaps when cut off from the ground, which is D's mechanism.","The claim is about confident-but-wrong answers and checking them against a real source file or a test that can fail, which is staying connected to the ground truth. It isn't about agent safety (H).","The claim is about facts: the AI sounds confident whether or not it's right, so check its output against a real source (a file or test result). That is staying connected to the ground truth.","The claim says the AI sounds confident whether or not it's right, so you should check it against a real source such as a file or a test result. That is grounding: staying connected to the truth when the AI fills gaps confidently and wrongly. C is about telling whether an agent is safe, which the claim doesn't cover.","The claim turns on checking the AI's output against a real source because it sounds equally confident right or wrong. That is grounding: stay connected to the truth, since a model that can't reach it fills the gap confidently and wrongly. H is about telling whether an agent is safe, which the claim doesn't mention.","The claim says to check the output against a real source because the AI sounds equally certain whether right or wrong. That is grounding: stay connected to the truth, because the AI fills gaps confidently and plausibly but wrongly."],"score":0.75,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-ops-director-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Check the AI's work with a separate check that looks at the sources, not by asking it 'are you sure'. And you can save that check as a file and run it on the next report.","claims":["When the AI hands you a report, don't just ask it to confirm itself. Have a second, independent check compare the report's claims against the original source material. Write that check down once as a reusable file so you can run it again on every later report.","When you need to trust something the AI produced, don't just ask it to confirm itself. Set up a separate review step that goes back to the original sources and compares the work against them. Save that review as a reusable file so you can run the same one on each new report.","When you need to trust something the AI produced, don't just ask it to confirm itself. Set up a separate review step that compares the output against the original source material. Write that review down as a reusable file so you can run the same check on the next report.","When the AI produces a report, don't ask it to confirm itself. Have a second, independent check compare the report's statements against the original sources, and keep that check written down in a reusable file so you can rerun it on every later report."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","evaluations","security","security","output-quality","output-quality","evaluations"],"why":["The claim is about checking the report's claims against the original source so the agent's output stays tied to the truth. That is grounding, not safety (C) or a reusable eval judge (B).","The claim is about checking a report's claims against the original source so unsupported gaps show up. That is grounding. It isn't about safety (G) or comparing versions with a fixed judge (C).","A fixed, reusable review file that compares output against original sources is a repeatable judge with defined criteria, which is the eval idea; the claim doesn't centre on grounding gaps (B) or on a generic safety check (C).","The claim is that you can't trust the output or the AI's own confirmation, so you build a separate, reusable check and run it each time. It doesn't say the AI fills gaps with plausible-sounding errors (F) or that you should measure changes against fixed criteria (C).","The claim is to verify AI output with a separate, reusable check you run, not by asking the AI to confirm itself. That matches 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'.","The claim is about checking AI output against the original source material instead of letting it vouch for itself. That is staying connected to the ground truth, and the reusable file only makes the check repeatable.","The claim is to check the report's statements against the original sources, which is staying connected to the ground truth. It isn't about measuring whether a change improved the work (E).","An independent, reusable, fixed check (written down, rerun on every report) that measures output against sources is the eval idea: repeatable criteria with a fixed judge."],"score":0.5,"hit":false,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-ops-director-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"ops-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"AI sounds just as sure when it makes things up, and asking 'are you sure' does not help. You need a check against a source file, and you can try a few checks, score them in a table and keep the best one to reuse.","claims":["Don't trust an AI's answer just because it sounds confident, and don't rely on asking it to double-check itself. Instead, verify its output against the original source material. Try several different ways of verifying, compare how well each one catches errors in a table, and keep the most reliable one so you can reuse it.","When the AI gives you facts, don't rely on asking it to double-check itself, because it will sound equally confident whether it is right or inventing things. Instead, compare its output against a real source document. Try several different ways of doing that comparison, rate each one in a table, and keep the best as a reusable routine.","When the AI gives you facts, don't trust it just because it sounds confident, and don't ask it to confirm itself. Compare its output against the original source document. Write several different versions of that comparison, test each on the same examples, record how well each performs in a table, and reuse the best one every time.","AI states invented things in the same confident tone as true things, and asking it to confirm itself doesn't catch that. Next week, for any task where wrong output would matter, I'll build a check that compares the output against the actual source material. I'll try several versions of that check, record how each performs in a table, and keep the best one to reuse."],"picks":["output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality","output-quality"],"why":["The claim says to distrust confident answers and check the output against the original source, which is grounding. The 'try several checks, compare in a table, keep the best' part is only the exercise mechanics.","The claim's core is grounding: don't trust confident output, check it against the original source. The 'compare methods in a table' part is the exercise, not a different idea.","Self-checking sounds equally confident, so compare against a real source document: that is grounding, staying connected to truth rather than letting the agent fill gaps.","The claim says the AI sounds equally confident whether right or inventing, and tells you to check its output against a real source document. That is grounding. The rate-and-keep-the-best table is only the exercise format.","The claim is about confident invention when the AI isn't tied to source material, and the fix is to check output against the actual source. That is grounding. Trying several checks and keeping the best is a side detail.","The claim is about the agent filling gaps with confident, plausible invention, and the fix is a check against the actual source material, which is grounding.","The core act is checking the AI's confident facts against the original source instead of asking it to confirm itself, which is grounding. The 'test several versions and keep the best' part is only a way of choosing the check.","The core is to compare the output against the original source rather than trusting confident-sounding text or asking the AI to confirm itself. That is grounding. The several-versions table is only a way to build the check."],"score":1,"hit":true,"half":true}
{"id":"v0-veteran-director-r1","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust an AI's confident answer just because it sounds sure. Plant known errors in a test, run several checkers, and score them to see which one you can trust, since none of them catches everything.","claims":["When you need to know which of several automatic reviewers to rely on, don't just trust whichever sounds most certain. Deliberately insert mistakes you already know about into sample material, run every reviewer on it, and tally how many of the planted mistakes each one finds. Rely on the one with the best tally, and remember that even it will miss some.","When you need to know which of several automated reviewers to rely on, don't take any reviewer's confident verdict at face value. Seed your test material with mistakes you already know about, run every reviewer on it, and count how many of the planted mistakes each one finds. Rely on the one with the best catch rate, and expect that even the best one misses some.","When you need to know which of several automatic reviewers to rely on, seed the material with mistakes you already know about, run every reviewer on it, and count how many of the planted mistakes each one finds. Use the one that finds the most, and don't assume any single reviewer catches everything.","When you need to know which of several automated reviewers to rely on, don't take any one's confident verdict at face value. Deliberately insert mistakes you already know about into a test set, run every reviewer on it, and count how many of the planted mistakes each one finds. Rely on the one with the best catch rate, and expect that even it will miss some."],"picks":["none","evaluations","evaluations","security","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations","evaluations"],"why":["The claim is about choosing between reviewers by planting known mistakes and counting catches. E is about measuring output with a fixed judge to see whether a change helped, and no other idea covers testing the reviewers themselves.","Planting known mistakes and tallying catches is measuring reviewers (judges) against a fixed, repeatable yardstick. That is eval/judge calibration, not safety checking (G) or grounding (D).","Planting known mistakes and counting each reviewer's catches is a fixed, repeatable measure against criteria you define. You use it to compare options instead of trusting their confidence, which is what the eval idea says.","Don't trust a reviewer's confident verdict; run a check against known planted flaws and count catches. That is the 'run the check, not wait for certainty' practice, not an eval of whether a change improved the work.","It scores each reviewer (judge) repeatably against known planted flaws to decide which to trust, which is an eval with a fixed, measured standard.","The claim is about measuring reviewers (judges) against criteria you define, here planted known mistakes, repeatably, so you can choose which one to trust. That is the eval idea.","Planting known flaws and scoring each reviewer's catch rate is a repeatable test of the judge itself, which is the eval idea of a fixed, validated judge. It isn't about safety (E) or grounding (H).","The claim is about validating a judge against known answers (planted defects) so you can trust a fixed judge's measurements. That is the eval idea, not safety (G) or grounding (H)."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-veteran-director-r2","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust one checking method or the tool's own 'are you sure?': test several checkers against errors you already know, because each catches different things, and make the one you keep say what it misses. The rest was mostly the usual framework talk and homework.","claims":["Before you rely on any method for checking AI output, run several different checkers on mistakes you've already planted and know about. See which ones catch which mistakes, since each misses different things. Don't accept the AI's own 'are you sure?' as a check. Keep one checker, and have it state plainly what kinds of mistakes it can't catch.","Before relying on any way of checking the agent's work, try several different checkers on mistakes you already know are in the work, see which ones catch which mistakes, and for the checker you keep, write down what it fails to catch. Don't accept the agent's own 'are you sure?' as a check.","Before you rely on any way of checking the agent's work, try it against mistakes you've planted or already know about, and use more than one checker, since each misses different things; for whichever one you keep, write down what it can't catch. Don't accept the tool's own self-confirmation as a check.","Before relying on any way of checking the agent's work, try several different checkers on mistakes you already know are in the work and see which ones find them, since each finds different kinds. Don't accept the agent's own self-review as enough. For the checker you keep, write down what it fails to catch."],"picks":["security","security","security","security","evaluations","security","security","security"],"why":["Planting known mistakes and testing which checkers catch them, plus rejecting the AI's own 'are you sure?', is about running a real check because output alone can't show safety.","The claim is about verifying AI output by running a check that has been tested against planted, known flaws, and about not accepting the AI's own 'are you sure?' as that check. That matches 'you can't tell from the output, so run the check'. It says nothing about measuring whether a change improved the work (B) or about staying connected to ground truth (A).","The claim is about not trusting output or the agent's self-review and relying on a check you have tested against known flaws, and noting what it misses. That is the 'you need a way to check' idea, not B's fixed-judge measurement of whether a change improved the work.","The claim is about checking the agent's work with real checks, not its self-review, and knowing each check's blind spots. That matches 'you can't tell from the output, run the check', not fixed-judge measurement of whether a change improved the work.","The claim is about validating the judge itself. You test candidate checkers on seeded known mistakes, then keep one fixed and document its blind spots. That is the eval-judge discipline, not the safety idea of running a check.","The claim is about verifying the agent's work with a real check, not its self-assessment, and knowing what that check misses. That matches F (you can't judge safety from output, so run a check). It says nothing about measuring change over time with a fixed judge, which is B.","The claim is about checking the agent's work with checks you have tested against planted mistakes, not trusting the tool's self-report, and running the check. It says nothing about measuring whether a change improved the work, so it isn't G.","The claim is about verifying the agent's work with real checks (planted mistakes, several checkers, known blind spots, no self-confirmation). It doesn't mention a fixed judge or measuring whether a change improved the work, which is what H is about."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-veteran-director-r3","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't ask an AI 'are you sure?': to know whether it made something up you need a separate check, and you can test that by planting known errors and scoring several checkers to pick the best one by the numbers. The module was honest that its 30-claim toy is small and that the checker only catches some kinds of errors.","claims":["When you want to know whether an AI's output contains invented facts, don't just ask it to double-check itself. Use a separate checking step, and pick that checker by trying several on material where you've deliberately inserted mistakes you already know about, then choosing the one that catches the most. Keep in mind that a small test set and a checker that only finds some kinds of mistakes mean the result is a rough guide, not proof.","When you want to know whether an AI invented something, don't just ask it to confirm itself. Use a separate checking step, and pick that step by testing it: put in mistakes you already know about, run several candidate checkers, and keep the one that finds the most. Remember that a small test set and a checker that only catches certain kinds of mistakes limit how far you can trust the result.","When you want to know whether an AI's output contains invented facts, don't just ask the AI to double-check itself; use a separate verification step. To choose which verification method to use, plant errors you already know about in some sample material, run each candidate method on it, and pick the one that catches the most. Keep in mind that a small test set and a method that only catches some kinds of mistakes limit how far you can trust the result.","When you want to know whether an AI's output contains invented facts, don't just ask it to double-check itself. Use a separate checking step. To choose which checker to use, seed a test set with mistakes you already know about, run each candidate checker on it, and keep the one that finds the most. Remember that a small test set and a checker that only finds some types of mistakes limit how far you can trust the result."],"picks":["evaluations","none","security","security","evaluations","security","security","security"],"why":["The claim is about choosing a fixed checker by scoring candidates on a seeded test set with known mistakes. That is validating a judge, which is the eval idea, not the grounding idea (C) about connecting to truth.","The claim is about picking a hallucination checker by testing it on seeded errors. That is not grounding (G), not a fixed-judge eval for measuring change (C), and not a safety check (E).","The claim says not to trust the AI's own confirmation and to run a separate check, which is F. It never says to stay connected to a source of truth (E), and choosing a checker by testing it on known mistakes is not the same as G's fixed judge for measuring changes.","The claim says not to trust the AI's own say-so and to run a separate, tested check. That is D's idea that you can't judge from the output and have to run the check. It doesn't carry A's idea of staying connected to the ground truth.","The claim is about building a fixed checker (judge), validating it on known seeded errors and comparing candidates, with caveats on test-set size and coverage. That is eval methodology, not the broader grounding idea A.","The claim's action is to stop trusting the output or a self-check and run a separate check, validated on seeded errors, and accept a rough result instead of waiting for proof. It never says to connect the agent to ground truth (E), and it isn't about measuring whether a change improved the work (D).","The claim says the output and the AI's own double-check can't be trusted, so you need a separate check and should test the check itself. That is D's 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'. It says nothing about staying connected to ground truth (F) or fixed criteria and a fixed judge (G).","The claim says you can't trust the AI's own output or self-check, so you need a separate check and have to run it and test it on planted errors. That matches D's 'you need a way to check, and the practice is running the check'. It doesn't carry B's grounding idea or H's idea of measuring change with a fixed judge."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
{"id":"v0-veteran-director-r4","module":"output-quality","group":"v0","persona":"veteran-director","page_hash":"47b3287e024b8d46","answer":"Don't trust the agent's confident tone: plant known errors, test your checks against them, and let a score show what each check catches and misses. It's measured on your own material, not taken on faith.","claims":["Before relying on any check you've built for the agent's work, deliberately insert mistakes you already know about into sample material, run the check over it, and count how many it catches and how many slip through. Use your own documents for this and judge the check by that count, not by how sure the agent sounds.","Before you rely on any automated check of the agent's work, deliberately insert mistakes you already know about into sample material, run the check on it, and record how many of the planted mistakes it catches and misses. Use those results, from your own data, to decide which checks to keep, rather than assuming a check works because the agent sounds sure.","Before relying on any review step, deliberately seed the work with mistakes you already know about, run the review, and count how many of the seeded mistakes it finds and misses. Use that count, from your own real material, to decide how much to trust the review, instead of trusting the assistant because it sounds sure.","Before you rely on any review step, deliberately put mistakes you already know about into the work, run the step on it, and keep a tally of which planted mistakes it flagged and which it let through. Use that tally, from your own real material, to decide how much to trust each step, rather than trusting the agent because it sounds sure."],"picks":["evaluations","evaluations","security","security","security","security","security","security"],"why":["Planting known mistakes and measuring catch/miss rates to validate a check is eval calibration: fixed criteria, repeatable judge, measured on your own data.","Planting known mistakes, measuring catch/miss rates on your own data, and keeping checks on that basis is about measuring and validating a judge/check repeatably (eval calibration), not about safety checks (G) or grounding (F).","Seeding known mistakes and counting catches versus misses is running the check to validate it, instead of trusting the agent's confidence. That is the idea that output alone can't show safety and you have to run a check.","The claim is about not trusting how sure the agent sounds, and instead running the check on planted known flaws and counting catches and misses. That matches 'you can't tell by looking at output, run the check'. It says nothing about a fixed judge or comparing versions, which is H.","Planting known mistakes and tallying what the check catches is running a check rather than trusting output or the agent's confidence, which is idea D. It is not H, since no fixed judge or criteria measure whether a change improved the work.","Planting known mistakes and tallying which the review step catches is a way to check, rather than trusting how confident the output sounds. That is F's idea that you can't tell by looking and the practice is running the check.","The claim says not to trust the review because the assistant sounds sure. It says to run a concrete check with seeded known mistakes, which matches 'you can't tell from the output, so run the check'.","The claim says not to trust a review because it sounds sure, and to run a check with known seeded mistakes instead. That is A (you can't judge from the output, so run the check). D is about comparing changes with a fixed judge, which the claim doesn't mention."],"score":0,"hit":false,"half":false}
